Key Points to Remember
• 401 vs 403: 401 means missing/wrong credentials; 403 means authenticated but not allowed (for
example, CSRF blocking a POST/PUT/DELETE when csrf is not disabled).
• BCrypt: passwords are never stored in plain text; encoder.encode() hashes the password at startup
and Spring Security compares the hash on each request.
• One rule protects all verbs: /api/secure/** matches GET, POST, PUT and DELETE alike.
• H2 console permit is for development only; remove it (and enable the console only in dev) in
production.
• HTTP Basic sends credentials Base64-encoded (not encrypted) on every request, so always use
HTTPS in real deployments.
Conclusion
Spring Security was added to a Spring Boot REST application with an in-memory BCrypt-encoded user, a
SecurityFilterChain that leaves /api/public/** open and protects /api/secure/** with HTTP Basic
authentication, and a Note resource backed by H2 whose GET, POST, PUT and DELETE operations are
all secured.
